---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Header } from '../components/layout/Header';
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — SecurePass"
  description="How SecurePass generates passwords: Web Crypto randomness, rejection sampling, and fully local, auditable code."
  canonicalPath="/about"
>
  <Header client:load />
  <main class="sp-container max-w-3xl py-10">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-3xl">About SecurePass</h1>
    <div class="mt-6 space-y-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">
      <p>
        SecurePass is a password generator that runs entirely in your browser. There is no
        account to create, no server that sees your passwords, and no analytics watching
        what you generate. The page you are reading <em>is</em> the whole application.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">How generation works</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          Randomness comes from the <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Web Crypto API</strong>
          (<code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] text-ink">crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) — the same
          cryptographic source browsers use for TLS keys. <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] text-ink">Math.random()</code>
          is never used for anything secret.
        </li>
        <li>
          Random values are mapped to characters with <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">rejection sampling</strong>,
          so every character in a pool is equally likely (no modulo bias).
        </li>
        <li>
          When “require one character from each group” is on, the forced characters are
          <strong class="font-semibold text-ink"> shuffled into random positions</strong> with a cryptographic shuffle —
          they never sit in predictable spots.
        </li>
        <li>
          Passphrases draw from the bundled <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">EFF large word list</strong> (7,776 words,
          ~12.9 bits of entropy per word), also via the Web Crypto API.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">What the strength meter means</h2>
      <p>
        The meter shows an <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">estimated entropy</strong> in bits, computed from the
        password length and the effective character pool (or word-list size for passphrases).
        It is a way to compare settings — not a guarantee. Real-world security also depends
        on phishing resistance, password reuse, and whether a password has leaked before.
        SecurePass does not claim to make you “unhackable”; a password manager plus unique
        passwords per site remains the best practical advice.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Technology</h2>
      <p>
        Built with <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Astro</strong> (static-first pages), <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">React</strong> islands
        (only the interactive generator hydrates), <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">TypeScript</strong>,
        <strong class="font-semibold text-ink"> Tailwind CSS</strong>, and <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">Lucide</strong> icons. The generation logic
        is deliberately separated from the UI in <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em] text-ink">src/lib/</code>
        so it stays small, testable, and easy to audit.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Open source</h2>
      <p>
        The full source is available on{' '}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/cloudleapasxm/password-generator"
          class="font-medium text-accent underline decoration-accent/30 underline-offset-2 can-hover:hover:decoration-accent"
        >
          GitHub
        </a>
        . If you can read code, you can verify every claim on this page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
